186233087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486531 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486538 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Slope of the Tangent at a Point of a Function Involving Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186486541 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115193 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7995b377-1de4-5126-aa29-8cc30b4918f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1045 "Differentiating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1050 1320.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1050 1320.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115457 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b360f8db-39eb-5e92-8961-f79b03985c78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115528 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115528 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{3}$ if $f(x)=11\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c56fab41-d998-532e-9118-40493f1a0626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115528 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative using the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(11\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(11\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =11(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{3}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116045 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116045 #edb/ref 1061 12343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116045 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "67a2cf2e-cd73-5fbe-afd2-7ccbda8b8270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116045 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116046 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116046 #edb/ref 1061 12345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116046 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b5a6be3-f0ee-5a11-899b-4c0f4b56a9f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116046 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116047 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116047 #edb/ref 1061 12346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116047 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "474fcd01-1e90-567e-bf97-01c21b7fde43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116047 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116048 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116048 #edb/ref 1061 12347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116048 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a445eb0-fd86-5366-ac85-89b0a9dd9f22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116048 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116049 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116049 #edb/ref 1061 12348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116049 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a7ecd00-ae83-5c88-8839-738a06fb168f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116049 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116050 #edb/ref 1061 12349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116050 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c3a01dc4-5466-5597-861a-59279cd05ea5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116050 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116214 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116214 #edb/ref 1061 28127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116214 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39d5bd85-a6de-52b6-bc2a-2fd13f7f99ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116214 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116473 #edb/ref 1064 "The derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sec x$ | $\\sec x\\tan x$ |\n| $\\csc x$ | $-\\csc x\\cot x$ |\n| $\\cot x$ | $-\\csc^2 x$ |\n\nAs with the standard trigonometric functions, we can now differentiate any combination of the above functions using the rules of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of \n\n$$\ny= \\sec x + \\csc x.\n$$\n\nWe can take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We could have computed the derivative of the above function by rewriting it as\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} + \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\nand applying the appropriate derivative rules. However, this would have taken significantly more work! You can save a lot of time by memorizing the derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116473 #edb/ref 1065 2629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116473 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "edab1db9-4d65-5f51-bae3-88df8fcf9c4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116473 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116474 #edb/ref 1064 "To see why the derivative of $\\sec x$ is $\\sec x \\tan x,$ we start by remembering that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nIf we now differentiate the above relation using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same method to find the derivative of the other two reciprocal trigonometric functions,\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116474 #edb/ref 1065 2630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116474 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "251c3450-3554-5f82-970a-27a2dfd156fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116474 #edb/ref 1067 "Justifying the Derivatives of Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116524 #edb/ref 1064 "Trigonometric functions appear very frequently in calculus, so it's extremely important to commit their derivatives to memory.\n\nConveniently, there are some mnemonic tricks that we can use to more easily remember the derivatives of trigonometric functions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) & =-{csc}^{2}⁡x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to notice that the \"co\"-functions (cosine, cosecant, and cotangent) all have negative signs in their derivatives, while the other trigonometric functions (sine, secant, and tangent) do not.\n\nIt's also helpful to realize that functions come in pairs.\n\n- Sine and cosine are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Tangent and secant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Cotangent and cosecant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\nLastly, it's helpful to realize the following:\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, tangent and cotangent have a similar format for their derivatives: the square of the function they're paired with.\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, secant and cosecant have a similar format for their derivatives: the product of themself and the function they're paired with." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116524 #edb/ref 1065 4574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116524 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cf33a5e8-2d8b-5494-b60a-fec2fa3cbe4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116524 #edb/ref 1067 "Mnemonic Tricks" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233781 #edb/ref 1069 "q-31288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233781 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233781 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ if $f(x)=-6\\operatorname{cot}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233781 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73219a58-c08c-520e-96a1-4134d001c0d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233781 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\operatorname{cot}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =-6(-{csc}^{2}x) \\\\  & =6{csc}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =6{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6{(2)}^{2} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285080 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{1}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285080 #